The content script runs in the context of the page it is injected into, while the popup script runs in the context of the popup window. They are in different contexts and cannot directly access each other's variables.

To share data between the content script and the popup script, you can use the chrome.runtime.sendMessage API. Here's an example of how you can do it:

In popup.js, you can listen for messages from the content script and handle them accordingly:

chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((request, sender, sendResponse) => {
// Handle the message from the content script
// You can access the session and CSRF token data here
});

In your content script, you can send a message to the popup script:

chrome.runtime.sendMessage({action: 'get_data'}, (response) => {
// Handle the response from the popup script
});

This way, the content script can send a message to the popup script, and the popup script can respond with the session and CSRF token data.

Note that you need to make sure that the popup script is listening for messages from the content script before the content script sends the message. You can do this by adding a listener for chrome.runtime.onMessage in the popup script before the content script sends the message.

Also, make sure that the popup script is not closed before the content script sends the message. If the popup script is closed, the message will not be received.

